  o Minor features (metrics port, relay):
    - Count onion service descriptor fetches and uploads on HSDirs by outcome,
      as tor_relay_hsdir_fetch_total and tor_relay_hsdir_upload_total. Uploads
      that are valid but not newer than the cached descriptor, which happens
      without any fault when several instances publish for the same address,
      are counted apart from invalid ones. Part of ticket 41339.
    - Count each tunneled directory request once, by what it asked for, as
      tor_relay_begindir_request_total{type}. A request dropped unread because
      its stream or circuit was already closing is counted as type
      "circuit_dead", and one too large to read, such as an oversized
      descriptor upload, as "unparsed". Count the tunneled directory responses
      fully handed to their stream, as
      tor_relay_begindir_response_total{type="hs_descriptor"|"other"}. Part of
      ticket 41339.
    - Report the tunneled directory connections a relay has open, by state, as
      the gauge tor_relay_begindir_connections. These connections have no
      socket, so tor_relay_connections doesn't show them. Part of ticket 41339.
    - Count the onion service directory requests whose previous hop is not a
      known relay, as tor_relay_dos_total{type="hsdir_unknown_prev_hop"},
      among those over an anonymous circuit, and the first on it. Stock clients
      and services use an unknown previous hop only while our consensus lags
      theirs. Part of ticket 41339.
    - Count CREATE cells, as tor_relay_create_cell_total, BEGIN_DIR cells, as
      tor_relay_begindir_cell_total, and tunneled directory requests, in
      tor_relay_begindir_request_total, by how their circuit's previous hop
      identified itself: "client" (it never authenticated: a tor client or a
      bridge), "known_relay" or "unknown_relay". This is not a hop count. Count
      BEGIN_DIR cells also by their place among their circuit's BEGIN_DIR cells
      (nth). Part of ticket 41339.
    - Count the CREATE cells from client channels that find their address's
      circuit creation bucket empty, as
      tor_relay_dos_total{type="circuit_over_rate"}, marked or not, and those
      from client addresses that have no bucket, as tor no longer tracks them
      or, on a bridge, tracks them under a pluggable transport, as
      {type="circuit_untracked"}, both while DoSCircuitCreationEnabled is on.
      Together with the client CREATE cells, they bound the number of client
      addresses from below. Part of ticket 41339.
    - Count the bytes of each tunneled directory response that its stream reads
      while the directory connection is open, by what the request asked for, as
      tor_relay_begindir_response_bytes_total. Part of ticket 41339.
    - Count CREATE and BEGIN_DIR cells by previous hop and by their place among
      the cells of the same kind that their source sent in the same minute
      (nth), as tor_relay_create_cell_source_total and
      tor_relay_begindir_cell_source_total. A source is a relay's channel, a
      client address that tor tracks, or else a client channel. The first band
      counts sources per minute, and the later bands show how much a limit per
      source would cut. Bridges don't count these, and other relays count them
      only while a MetricsPort is configured. Part of ticket 41339.
    - Count onion service descriptor fetches that reach the HSDir cache by
      whether the descriptor was found (lookup) and by their place among the
      fetches of the same descriptor in the same minute (nth), as
      tor_relay_hsdir_fetch_descriptor_total. This tells an operator whether a
      flood fetches a few descriptors again and again or sweeps many. Only a
      keyed hash of each key is kept, until a fetch is counted in a later
      minute. Bridges don't count these, and other relays count them only while
      a MetricsPort is configured. Part of ticket 41339.
    - Count the onion service directory requests that tor accepts whose
      previous hop is a relay we know only by its descriptor, not in our
      consensus, as tor_relay_dos_total{type="hsdir_descriptor_only_prev_hop"}.
      HSDirRefuseUnknownPrevHop treats these as known: the count shows how much
      traffic comes from relays that recently left the consensus. Part of
      ticket 41339.
    - Count the tunneled directory requests of types "no_request" and
      "circuit_dead" by the first cause we noted for their closing, as
      tor_relay_begindir_close_reason_total{type,prev_hop,reason}: a
      RELAY_END, a DESTROY, a closed channel, a protocol violation, the
      BEGIN_DIR rate limit, an onion service request defense, a full cell
      queue, the out-of-memory handler, a stall, or another reason closing
      its circuit. Part of ticket 41339.
